A||||8||Greg Piet||cracked exhaust manifold||||8
Z||000000||Greg Piet||09-12-1999||03:56 AM||||I have a cracked exhaust manifold and the other one has a pinhole in it from a crappy re-weld.  I have seen that ported headers give a boost of about 8 hp, but how much of a power decrease should there be for the above mentioned conditions?  (really dramatic, minor performance loss)?<BR>It has recently gone bad, and im not sure if its my imagination or not, but my v6 feels embarassingly slower now.||||reg||8
Z||000001||batboy||09-12-1999||11:20 AM||||Unless the leaking exhaust manifolds are causing a problem with the oxygen sensor, (are you getting a check engine light) there shouldn't really be any real performance drop. Maybe a little less back pressure which might drop the torque down a bit. My experience with exhaust leaks is that it sounds so bad and loud that I drive easier and slower. I just got the exhaust fixed on my Suburban and now I can jump down on the gas again without worrying about waking up the neighborhood. It just seems like I have more power now, but really it's about the same.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-12-1999||11:45 AM||||I'd be more worried about it spitting fire and lighting up the car in some rather not desirable way.||||reg||11
Z||000003||88 Fiero||09-12-1999||11:59 AM||||just replace it with a new set of exhaust manifolds or replace it with a set of headers and not to worry about cracks.....||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||09-12-1999||04:42 PM||||I also have an exhaust leak and it seems to be more an embarrasment (sounds like a VW)<BR>than an actual performance decrease.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bruce||09-12-1999||09:40 PM||||Greg,<BR>Did you ever have the recall work done on the manifolds?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||09-12-1999||09:51 PM||||I have no idea!<BR>I got eh car with 100k miles on it.  What recall work is that?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bruce||09-12-1999||11:43 PM||||Because of engine fires, there was a recall of Fieros, at least up to the 86 models. the recall included replacing or repairing the manifold, the emergency brake, some of the heat shields, and the rear brake calipers. <BR>After this work was done, a sticker was placed under the front deck lid. Check to see if you have one of those stickers. It has the last two digits of your model year and two other numbers. If you're not sure whether or not the work was done, call your local Pontiac number and give them your VIN #. They can tell you what was or was not done. <BR>The work is free and mandated by GM, so don't take any guff!! ||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||09-13-1999||12:51 AM||||Go to a Pontiac dealer with your VIN and ask for all "campains" (sp?). I believe the shield/PCV/manifold is a "warranty always" regardless of the current owner. <P>By law, they must give you the information. Keep in mind that if their system shows the work was done previously, you are SOL.||||reg||1
